1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,951
What if the Moon moved closer every night?

2
00:00:02,991 --> 00:00:04,517
Tonight it looks normal.

3
00:00:04,557 --> 00:00:06,475
Tomorrow it's a little bigger.

4
00:00:06,515 --> 00:00:08,824
By the weekend, kids are using it as

5
00:00:08,864 --> 00:00:11,239
a nightlight, and nobody is sleeping.

6
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:15,040
On night three, the tides get serious.

7
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:16,905
The sea creeps up the beach,

8
00:00:16,945 --> 00:00:20,567
sandcastles surrender, and a very confused crab wonders

9
00:00:20,607 --> 00:00:22,165
who moved the shoreline.

10
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:23,402
Night five.

11
00:00:23,442 --> 00:00:25,418
The moon fills half the sky.

12
00:00:25,458 --> 00:00:27,650
Waves roll through the streets,

13
00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:29,378
cars bob like bath toys,

14
00:00:29,418 --> 00:00:32,258
and the pigeons file a formal complaint.

15
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:33,464
Night seven.

16
00:00:33,504 --> 00:00:36,227
Scientists say the real tidal pull would rip

17
00:00:36,267 --> 00:00:38,426
oceans upward and shake continents.

18
00:00:38,466 --> 00:00:40,122
In this thought experiment,

19
00:00:40,162 --> 00:00:43,074
the whole planet trembles like a nervous jelly.

20
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:45,243
Then comes the Roche limit,

21
00:00:45,283 --> 00:00:47,488
where gravity tears the Moon apart.

22
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:50,568
It cracks, wobbles, and bursts into ten thousand

23
00:00:50,608 --> 00:00:53,456
glowing pebbles that scatter across the dark.

24
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:55,358
The pebbles don't fall.

25
00:00:55,398 --> 00:00:58,317
They spread into a glittering ribbon around the

26
00:00:58,357 --> 00:01:00,520
Earth, orbiting in perfect circles,

27
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:03,857
like a necklace someone dropped across the whole sky.

28
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:07,121
Twist: the Earth gets rings, like Saturn.

29
00:01:07,161 --> 00:01:09,863
At night they glow across the whole sky,

30
00:01:09,903 --> 00:01:11,577
bright enough to read by.

31
00:01:11,617 --> 00:01:14,593
The planet that lost its Moon found a crown.

32
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:16,751
Night thirty.

33
00:01:16,791 --> 00:01:18,560
The kids lie on the roof,

34
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:22,830
counting glittering pebbles that drift like slow fireflies.

35
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:24,061
The Moon is gone,

36
00:01:24,101 --> 00:01:26,811
but the sky has never looked so close,

37
00:01:26,851 --> 00:01:27,607
or so kind.
